[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the shared validation module be loaded? → ES modules (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m ES modules it is.

  [1mThe fork:[0m today [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m returns [38;5;153m{ valid: false, error: "Missing required fields" }[39m — one string, no indication of
  which field failed. That return shape is the interface every future form consumes, so it's the expensive thing to
  change later.

  - [1mPer-field error map[0m — [38;5;153m{ valid, errors: { username: "Username is required" } }[39m, collecting every failure rather than
    stopping at the first. Lets each form render a message next to the offending input. Cost: the call site in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
    changes, and it's marginally more code.
  - [1mKeep the single string[0m — smallest possible diff, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m untouched. Cost: no form can ever say [3mwhich[0m field is wrong
    without a breaking change to the shared module — and breaking it gets more expensive with each form added, which is
    exactly the situation you're trying to avoid.
  - [1mBoth[0m — [38;5;153m{ valid, error, errors }[39m, [38;5;153merror[39m being the first message. Backward compatible today. Cost: two representations
    of the same truth that can drift, for a compatibility burden of exactly one call site.

  [1mMy recommendation: the per-field map.[0m With one consumer, changing [38;5;153mapp.js[39m costs a few lines; the single-string shape is
  cheap now and is the one decision here that gets genuinely hard to reverse. I'd skip "both" — preserving
  compatibility for a single call site you control isn't worth carrying two shapes.
